[{:db/id -1
  :adr/id "2608052000"
  :adr/title "Records cross the native parameter boundary: murakumo's shipped Kotoba cores compile to machine code (0/33 → 14/33)"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-08-05"
  :adr/tags ["kotoba" "compiler" "native" "murakumo" "aiueos" "records" "evidence"]
  :adr/supersedes ""
  :adr/superseded-by ""
  :adr/deciders ["Jun Kawasaki"]
  :adr/authoritative true
  :adr/source-format "edn"
  :adr/context
  "ADR-2608051200 closed the aiueos USB-boot work and left five named gaps. The owner asked for gaps 1 and 2 to be designed and implemented. This ADR records gap 2; gap 1 (no network stack on bare metal) is NOT implemented and is restated below so it is not mistaken for done.

Gap 2 was: murakumo's shipped pure-planner Kotoba cores do not compile to any aiueos/native target, so the bare-metal node had to carry a hand-written MIRROR of murakumo.infer.join rather than the real thing.

The cause was one restriction, not a deep property of the machine model. ADR-0062 (kotoba-lang/compiler) gave the native backends a record with NO runtime representation: a let-bound record flattens into one stack slot per field, and a record RESULT boxes into a `pair` chain because a record is N slots and a function returns one word. A record PARAMETER has exactly the same problem and had no route at all — both admission gates (kotoba.kir and, independently, kotoba.verifier) capped param-types at #{:i64 :string}, and both backends threw \"record-get is only supported directly over a matching record-new construction\".

Measured 2026-08-05 against murakumo's 33 shipped *_core.kotoba modules — which already compile for wasm32, js and cljs and ship as precompiled KIR: 0 of 33 compiled for x86_64-kotoba-v1, and every signature failure was a record parameter, an [:option T] record field, or a :bool result. Never anything in a body."

  :adr/decision
  "A record crosses INTO a function exactly the way it already crossed OUT: boxed into the ADR-0062 pair chain. No new representation, no ABI change, no new primitive, no loader change — both directions are source rewrites into forms the backends already emit.

Landed across four repos, in dependency order:
- kotoba-kir a54916b — the admission gate admits :i64/:string/:keyword, [:option T]/[:result T E] and records at a function boundary; [:option T] also becomes an admissible record FIELD (it is already one word).
- kotoba-native 9b33db5 — both ISAs: a projection whose operand is a parameter walks the chain; emit-call boxes a record argument, either from a literal record-new or by re-boxing a flattened let-bound record from its slots in field order.
- kotoba-verifier 400d980 — the same boundary set, independently re-derived as this file does for every shape, plus the LIBRARY module shape (no entry, no signature, non-empty exports) beside the entry shape.
- compiler 91c943e — entryless libraries compile on native targets whose artifact needs no entry point; ADR-0219 carries the full record.

Two things were deliberately NOT done, and both are recorded rather than quietly skipped:

1. A bare :bool PARAMETER stays rejected, preserving both gates' prior behaviour exactly. Measured: kotoba.kir/execute itself refuses one with {:trap :value-type-mismatch :expected :i64 :position {:parameter b}}. That gap is in the INTERPRETER, not either backend. Admitting the type while the oracle cannot run one would ship a boundary nothing had executed.

2. KIR signatures are NOT ref-expanded. The first attempt expanded [:ref :ns/name] in `lower` so the verifier could resolve it — cleaner, and reverted: :kir-sha256 digests exactly the select-keys'd program, so expansion moved the digest of every module using a schema reference ON EVERY TARGET, including its wasm bytes. Caught by golden-digests-match-live-compile drifting on :composed-surface-kit. The verifier instead admits the reference by NAME and requires a projection to be a well-formed record whose own name equals it, so projecting schema A through a parameter declared as a reference to B is still rejected."

  :adr/consequences
  "14 of murakumo's 33 shipped cores now compile to real x86-64 machine code, from 0. infer_join_core emits 2,041 bytes with all 8 exports. The remaining 19 fail on other typed features still outside the slice (11) or on a record-new in a position the backends cannot yet place (4) — named, not claimed as done.

Every widened shape is EXECUTED, not argued. The shared ISA execution table gains record parameters (first field, second field, forwarded through a second function, re-boxed from a let, and a record result fed straight in as a parameter), keyword/option/result parameters, and option-typed record fields in both some and none cases — as real processes on BOTH ISAs: 185 assertions, 0 failures. Rows select DIFFERENT fields on purpose, because a chain walked to the wrong depth still returns a plausible i64.

The table's shared-across-ISAs design earned itself again: the x86-64 half was written first, passed every row, and aarch64 threw — exactly the divergence two per-ISA tables would hide.

Consequence for aiueos: os/aiueos/kotoba/murakumo-join-plan.kotoba (ADR-2608051200) is now a MIRROR OF SOMETHING THAT COULD BE USED DIRECTLY. It is deliberately not retired in this change — the aiueos kernel-object ABI exports through an allow-list in kotoba-native, and admitting an upstream core as an aiueos APPLICATION still needs the runtime-v2 capability context and the offline signer. Retiring the mirror is a follow-on with its own evidence.

Suites: kotoba-kir 470 assertions green, kotoba-verifier 65 green, kotoba-native 2,152 green. Compiler: 6,870 assertions / 30 failures + 1 error before, 7,017 / the same 30 failures / 0 errors after — those 30 are pre-existing on unmodified origin/main in this environment (GitHub Actions was removed by ADR-2607300900, so nothing else catches them), and the baseline error was the new ISA rows correctly failing against unpatched libraries."

  :adr/gaps
  "[{:gap :bare-metal-network :state :not-implemented :note \"GAP 1 OF THE REQUEST, NOT DONE. Reaching murakumo.cloud needs virtio-net + ARP/IPv4 + TCP + TLS + HTTPS. aiueos PCI enumerates virtio RNG/BLK/INPUT/GPU only; there is no NIC driver and no stack. The next concrete step is a virtio-net driver following the existing virtio-blk pattern in os/aiueos/kernel/pci.c, with packet/descriptor admission as a Kotoba object and a QEMU gate proving real TX/RX -- link layer only, with IP/TCP/TLS staged above it.\"}
    {:gap :remaining-murakumo-cores :state :partial :note \"SUPERSEDED BY MEASUREMENT 2026-08-05. The '11 typed features / 4 record-new' split was a first-pass guess. Re-swept on both ISAs against kotoba-native b65fd0d (identical failure sets on x86_64 and aarch64): 16/33 compile, and the 17 failures collapse into exactly THREE holes, not seventeen increments -- 9 on string-contains? / string-replace-all having no native lowering (deploy_plan, fleet_inventory, kekkai_gate, overlay_crypto, persist, provision_plan, reconcile_plan, secret, tunnel), 8 on a bare :bool PARAMETER (connect, dash_state, infer_schedule, infer_waste, overlay_stream, reconcile_plan, report, task_plan), 1 on record-get over a let-bound boxed handle (infer_plan, rejected by kotoba-verifier). reconcile_plan is in two classes. Two cores were closed by widening record-new tail placement: infer_credits and infer_rebalance.\"}
    {:gap :bool-parameter :state :partial :note \"THE DIAGNOSIS BELOW WAS WRONG; corrected 2026-08-05 by measurement. It is NOT that kotoba.kir/execute refuses a :bool argument. execute correctly refuses a host boolean where the KIR it was handed DECLARES :i64 -- because that KIR carries no :param-types table, and invoke-function defaults an absent table to :i64 per parameter. The table is absent because lower keeps :param-types only for :kotoba.hir/v3 and the compiler frontend's typed-values? excludes :bool by name. Where the table IS present -- a :bool parameter beside any other typed feature, the shape this gate actually governs -- execute runs one and always could (9 of 10 new tests pass on the UNMODIFIED interpreter). The real exclusion was a single (not= :bool type) guard in kotoba.kir/native-boundary-type?; native-word-value-type? had listed :bool since it was written. Removed in kotoba-kir 8de6215a. kotoba-verifier independently re-derives the same boundary set and still needs the identical one-line removal -- measured in-process, that alone takes the end-to-end result from :error :verify to {:ok true :target :x86_64-kotoba-v1} with a .kexe written, and NOTHING in kotoba-native needs changing. Residual: verify-native-artifact! re-executes through the KIR interpreter, not the emitted code, so a :bool parameter has still never been RUN as machine code.\"}
    {:gap :bool-untyped-encoding :state :open :note \"Opened 2026-08-05, split out of the corrected :bool-parameter entry. A module whose ONLY typed feature is a :bool parameter is emitted as :kotoba.hir/v2 and loses :param-types on the way down, so execute defaults it to :i64. This is the compiler's typed-values? classification, and closing it moves :kir-sha256 for every affected module on every target including its wasm bytes -- so it is deliberately left open rather than fixed under an unrelated task. kotoba-kir carries a regression test pinning the trap so it stays measured rather than remembered.\"}
    {:gap :host-exception-crosses-language-boundary :state :open :note \"Found 2026-08-05 while proving the :bool boundary. An i64 op applied to a host boolean -- (+ b 1) on a :bool parameter -- escapes as a raw host ClassCastException instead of a Kotoba trap. Unreachable from source because the frontend type-checks it away, so it is not gated; but a host exception crossing the language boundary is a defect on its own terms. Recorded in kotoba-kir ADR-0221 so it is not rediscovered as a bool problem.\"}
    {:gap :aiueos-mirror-retirement :state :open :note \"murakumo-join-plan.kotoba can eventually be replaced by the upstream core; needs the kernel-object allow-list decision plus runtime-v2 context and signer.\"}]"

  :adr/evidence
  "kotoba-kir a54916bca005f9df5419190c6506d44adcd0311e, kotoba-native 9b33db546176557e8e95aa49dcaeedd09e9fee33, kotoba-verifier 400d980b183704e61c060dead445a2ffbf00fc52, compiler 91c943e876fa8a0786b3662e1d8f54532981bfa6 — all on main, all verified fast-forward via gh api compare before the west pins advanced in root 109866fb.
Native compile of murakumo infer_join_core: only-native-word? true, 2041 code bytes, exports (can? clamp-resident eligible-for-work? max-resident-bytes needs-relay? tier-browser tier-native tier-wasm).
Sweep of all 33 cores: 0/33 on unmodified main, 14/33 after — both measured with the same script.
Follow-on landings 2026-08-05, each re-measured rather than quoted: kotoba-native b65fd0d25e5a6e84ad7930c986c374bd5c1fc45a (record results box from any tail position; 14/33 -> 16/33 on BOTH ISAs with identical failure sets; suite 2152 -> 2218 assertions; 5 new rows on the shared ISA execution table = 205 assertions, 0 failures, no ISA skipped, and those rows abort on unmodified 78f3111 so they do test the change). kotoba-kir 8de6215a9ac8e07db2b80f8fa614572109cbf3b5 (:bool admitted at a function boundary; 470 -> 499 assertions; golden digests ok? true, 60 cases, unmoved). artifact 86d88bd58299cf575e9dfa2ab4789a0baa166614 (descriptor table carriage, ADR-2608049000 stage 1; 5 -> 59 assertions; every gate verified by breaking what it guards, one of which was initially a false green). All three verified fast-forward via gh api compare before the west pins advanced (root af513c82, cd1c0b25, af805a20). No capability kit qualification flag moved in any of them — all :native-aot remain :pending.
ISA execution: Ran 2 tests containing 185 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors, against the PUBLISHED pins rather than local overrides.
Golden digests: ok? true, 60 cases, after the ref-expansion revert."

